 ## [Unreleased]
 
-- Guards judge a Bash command whose head word is a shell alias from the Claude Code shell snapshot, such as `gpf! origin main`, as the git or gh command it expands to, as well as itself. With aliases loaded, the guards read the command's own simple commands exactly as without aliases, in the same order and context, then every expansion after all of them, so an alias can only add votes: it never removes a deny or an ask, and never gets an allow, which kern gives only to a command that starts with git or gh. The head word is found after assignments (quoted ones included), redirections and zsh's reserved words (`if`, `!`, `time` and the like), in each simple command, `$( )` and `eval`. An alias whose value ends in a space, such as `_='sudo '`, also expands the word after it, as zsh's trailing-space rule does, so `_ gpf! origin main` is judged as `sudo git push --force origin main`; past 8 such words, a word that is still an alias is denied under autopilot. G2, G3, G4, G13, G14 and G15, which skip a command whose text names no gh, push, deploy tool or network client, read the expansion's text too, so `prc` for `gh pr create`, `gp origin production` as a push to a deploy branch, and an alias that runs psql against a protected target are judged. kern reads the snapshot as text and never runs it; a missing, unreadable or unparseable snapshot changes nothing. This removes the 0.2.0 known limit on shell aliases. Known limits, left for M3: the newest snapshot stands in for the session's, which the hook input does not name; an alias defined and used in one Bash call through `eval`, `$( )` or a backtick is not judged interactively (G00 denies the definition under autopilot); nor is an alias past another alias's head word, a global alias, a `$'...'` or double-quoted alias value, a chain of aliases deeper than 8, or interactively an alias past 8 trailing-space words; an expansion no longer sets context for the command's later records, so `gaa && git commit -m x` loses G6's stale